      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays aren't just winning; they're roaring back with a swagger that feels dangerously close to contender energy. <strong>Kazuma Okamoto tied Shohei Ohtani's record for home runs by a Japanese-born rookie with a three-run blast in Friday's 5-3 victory over the Padres</strong>, cementing himself as the heartbeat of this three-game tear. The Jays sit at 45-49, third in the AL East and 11 games behind, but that W3 streak - culminating in a 10-0 shutout of the Giants and a 9-3 romp before it - suggests momentum is finally sticking. Okamoto's 22 homers, more than double anyone else on the team, are the story right now, a power surge translating NPB dominance into MLB impact. <strong>They hit San Diego again tonight at 8:40 PM ET, with Okamoto hunting No. 23 and the Jays chasing a chance to climb within single-digit distance</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated July 11, 2026"
      },
